// Cue notes example
// A cue is a REGION: `cue { … }` maps onto LilyPond's \new CueVoice { … }, whose
// fontSize = #-4 is a CONTEXT property. The last measure shows the clef form, which is
// LilyPond's \cueClef / \cueClefUnset — also a property of the region, not of a note.
// LILYPOND-REF: ly/engraver-init.ly CueVoice context — fontSize = #-4
// See docs/cue-context-design.md.
//
// ⚠️ "1:1" USED TO BE WRITTEN HERE AND IT IS TRUE ONLY WHERE THIS BOOK PUTS ITS CUES.
// A `\new CueVoice { … }` ends its MUSIC, not its CONTEXT, so notes after it join the cue
// when there is no earlier Voice to return to — i.e. when the block is the staff's FIRST
// music. MEASURED (audit/lp-geometry/probes/voice-boundary-spacing.ly): VB-FIRST prints
// four cue-sized heads where VB-AFTER prints two, same music otherwise; and VB-TWIN, which
// is THIS staff's `lysc ly` output pasted verbatim, reads full, full, cue, cue, FULL, FULL,
// cue, cue — identical to the spelling with an explicit \new Voice. Every cue here has notes
// before it, so the twin is sound. A cue written first in a part is not, and is open.
//
// ⚠️ The second part used to be called `chords`, which became a reserved word when the
// chord-track grammar landed; this fixture had not parsed since (LYS0002) and nothing
// noticed, because it had no snapshot. It has one now — that hole was the whole lesson,
// and until 2026-08-03 test/cue-accidentals was still the only cue DRAWING in the corpus,
// which is how the cue spacing law came to be watched by one ledger point and nothing else.
//
// ✔ VERIFIED TO GUARD, not assumed to. Both halves of the cue spacing law were switched off
// in turn and this book fails for each:
//   ApplyLeftHeadWidth asking Design20 again (the head-width term)     201 pass / 2 fail
//   CrossesVoiceBoundary disabled (the voice-boundary term)            201 pass / 2 fail
// ⚠️ AND IT WATCHES THEM MORE DIRECTLY THAN test/cue-accidentals DOES. There, every column
// inside a measure is bound by an accidental rod, so the head-width term only reaches the
// drawing at the cue → barline gap. Here the cue notes carry no accidentals, so the
// cue → cue step IS the refined ideal: measured 14.49 → 17.00 = 2.51, LilyPond's
// 2.513393907138009, and the main → cue step 11.59 → 14.49 = 2.90 is its unrefined
// 2.898044999134612. The two numbers this fixture prints are the two the law predicts.

title "Cue Notes Demo"
tempo 120
time 4/4
key c major

part melody
part harmony

// Single notes: normal, then a cue region (each measure: 2 normal + 2 cue)
phrase cueMelody {
  c4 d cue { e4 f } |
  g4 a cue { b4 c' } |
}

// Chords: normal, then a cue region; then a cue region in another clef
phrase cueChords {
  <c, e g>4 <d f a>4 cue { <e g b>4 <f a c'>4 } |
  <c e g>2 cue bass { <c, e, g,>2 } |
}

section Main {
  melody { cueMelody }
  harmony { cueChords }
}

form main { Main }

score main "cue-melody" {
  staff treble melody
}

score main "cue-chords" {
  staff treble harmony
}
